1. what the early declarations really meant


&nbsp;


in 1776, the united states wrote its declaration of independence.


&nbsp;


in 1789, france wrote its declaration of the rights of man and of the citizen.


&nbsp;


both documents said that all men were created equal.


&nbsp;


but they did not mean everyone.


&nbsp;


who was left out?


&nbsp;


- women


- enslaved people


- anyone who was not a white man of european descent


&nbsp;


the rights in these documents only served free white men.


&nbsp;


those rights included:


&nbsp;


1. the right to own property


2. freedom of expression


3. the right to take part in government


&nbsp;


a small group of radical thinkers read the documents differently.


&nbsp;


they argued that women should have the same rights as men.


&nbsp;


they also argued that people of all races deserved equal rights and social standing.


&nbsp;


most people did not agree with them.


&nbsp;


still, these documents had a real effect.


&nbsp;


they partly inspired a slave revolt in saint-domingue.


&nbsp;


this revolt became one of the most important results of the french revolution in the americas.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


2. saint-domingue: a colony built on enslaved labor


&nbsp;


saint-domingue was a french colony.


&nbsp;


it sat on the western side of the caribbean island of hispaniola.


&nbsp;


france founded it in the mid-1600s.


&nbsp;


soon, it became the most profitable colony in the americas.


&nbsp;


this profit came mostly from enslaved african labor.


&nbsp;


what happened to enslaved people on the island?


&nbsp;


they were brought there by force.


&nbsp;


they worked on sugar plantations.


&nbsp;


they worked on coffee plantations.


&nbsp;


they worked on indigo plantations.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


3. the revolt begins (1791)


&nbsp;


in 1791, freed and enslaved africans rose up.


&nbsp;


other islanders of african descent joined them.


&nbsp;


two leaders stood out:


&nbsp;


- toussaint louverture


- jean-jacques dessalines


&nbsp;


both had been born enslaved on local plantations.


&nbsp;


what happened next?


&nbsp;


the revolt grew into a full revolution.


&nbsp;


it led to the colony's independence.


&nbsp;


in 1804, haiti declared itself a free republic.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


4. what made haiti unique


&nbsp;


the haitian revolution was unlike any other in the americas.


&nbsp;


here is why.


&nbsp;


in the british colonies of north america, white colonists fought for political representation and greater economic freedom.


&nbsp;


they were already near the top of the social order.


&nbsp;


in saint-domingue, it was different.


&nbsp;


the fight for independence was led by people at the very bottom of the social hierarchy.


&nbsp;


enslaved and formerly enslaved people drove the revolution forward.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


5. two goals, two kinds of freedom


&nbsp;


the rebellion in haiti had two goals.


&nbsp;


it sought two forms of liberation.


&nbsp;


goal one – for free black people and mixed-race elites:


&nbsp;


freedom meant an end to colonialism.


&nbsp;


they wanted independence from french rule.


&nbsp;


goal two – for enslaved black people:


&nbsp;


liberation was more basic.


&nbsp;


it meant freedom from abuse on the plantation.


&nbsp;


it meant an end to slavery itself.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


6. victory in 1804


&nbsp;


by january 1804, black revolutionaries had won.


&nbsp;


here is how:


&nbsp;


they used successful guerrilla tactics.


&nbsp;


the french army was already weakened by an outbreak of yellow fever.


&nbsp;


the revolutionaries decimated it.


&nbsp;


haiti became a nation.


&nbsp;


it was the second liberated territory in the western hemisphere to have a republican system.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


7. fear spreads across the americas


&nbsp;


after haiti's uprising, political elites across the americas felt fear.


&nbsp;


they worried that haiti's success would inspire other slave rebellions.


&nbsp;


who might rise up next?


&nbsp;


- american indians


- free and enslaved africans


- people of mixed-racial ancestry


&nbsp;


this fear had a big effect.


&nbsp;


it made the creole aristocracy hesitant.


&nbsp;


they did not want to imitate the liberty-seeking anglo-american colonists.


&nbsp;


the haitian example dampened their support for independence from their parent countries, rather than arousing it.


&nbsp;


instead, upheavals on the iberian peninsula itself would break the ties between spain and portugal and their colonies.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


