# the calm before the storm


&nbsp;

## table of contents


- a long peace
- prussia and austria go to war (1866)
- the franco-prussian war (1870–1871)
- why these wars felt "safe"


&nbsp;



&nbsp;

## a long peace


&nbsp;

the decades before world war i were mostly peaceful. people called that war "the great war." europe had been stable for a long time.


&nbsp;

in 1815, european leaders made a plan to keep power balanced. this came after the napoleonic wars. it worked well.


&nbsp;

they formed something called **the concert of europe**. this was an informal agreement. its goal: keep things the same. the concert handled some internal conflicts. it also took in new, more liberal political ideas. neither caused major trouble.


&nbsp;

there were a few military clashes. but they were short. and small in scale.


&nbsp;



&nbsp;

## prussia and austria go to war (1866)


&nbsp;

prussia was different from most nations. it had a culture built around the army. prussia still saw war as a tool to get what it wanted. france felt the same way.


&nbsp;

in 1866, **austria-hungary** went to war with **prussia**. the conflict lasted about seven weeks. prussia won easily. why? prussia had put money into better military technology and weapons. after the war, prussia took over several german states.


&nbsp;

this changed everything for austria:


&nbsp;

- austria was kept out of german affairs.
- prussia became the leading german state.
- the war pushed austria into a **dual monarchy** system — an alliance with hungary.


&nbsp;

austria agreed to this alliance because it needed partners after losing its role in german politics. but the setup was hard to manage. austria and hungary each kept their own national government and parliament. they had different goals. yet they shared one empire.


&nbsp;



&nbsp;

## the franco-prussian war (1870–1871)


&nbsp;

this war was also brief, but it caused major change.


&nbsp;

france declared war on prussia. why? a dispute over who would inherit the spanish throne. the fighting lasted only a few months. but the results were severe:


&nbsp;

- the prussian army surrounded paris.
- napoléon iii was captured.
- france had to pay a large **war indemnity** to prussia.
- france also paid for the occupation forces stationed in its own land.
- france lost the territories of **alsace and lorraine**. (these regions changed hands several times during the first half of the twentieth century.)


&nbsp;

for france, this was a humiliating defeat. but it opened the door to something bigger: the unification of the german empire.


&nbsp;

the treaty was signed in the **hall of mirrors** at the palace of versailles. this act locked in prussia's leadership of the new empire. it also finished what the revolutions of 1848 had left undone. germany emerged as a major power in europe.


&nbsp;



&nbsp;

## why these wars felt "safe"


&nbsp;

the fighting in these wars stayed local. the wars were short. so no country needed to pull its full economy or all its resources into the fight. they did not involve many nations. the fighting rarely crossed into other countries' lands.


&nbsp;

with one exception — the franco-prussian war — the impact on civilian life was small. few civilians died. little property was destroyed.


&nbsp;

in a sense, this pattern gave europe a false sense of comfort. people expected that any future war would be just as quick and small.

# table of contents


- why britain left india


- the split and the violence


- balancing between two sides in the cold war


- the problem with china's border


---


&nbsp;


## why britain left india


&nbsp;


in **1947**, the united kingdom gave india its independence.


&nbsp;


but why did britain let go? a few things pushed it out:


&nbsp;


**protests shook the country.**
all through 1946, people in india protested against british rule. the anger was loud and constant.


&nbsp;


**britain was worn out.**
world war two had drained its money and its strength. running a colony like india simply cost too much. britain could not afford it anymore.


&nbsp;


**the soldiers were no longer loyal to britain.**
the united kingdom still kept troops in india, but most of those troops were indian men. nobody really knew which side they would stand on. in 1946, indian sailors serving in the british navy **mutinied** across the country. that was a clear warning sign.


&nbsp;


**the united states pushed britain to let go.**
the united states was britain's biggest ally and its main lender of money. it had already given independence to the philippines after the war. it told britain: it is time.


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## the split and the violence


&nbsp;


when people heard that the british were leaving, **waves of religious violence** swept through the land.


&nbsp;


the core question was this:

what would an independent india look like?


&nbsp;


hindus in the indian national congress wanted **one united india**. everyone together, one country.


&nbsp;


muslims feared that a hindu majority would take over the government and rule against them. many muslims refused to accept that kind of arrangement. they did not feel safe under it.


&nbsp;


a compromise was reached.


&nbsp;


at **midnight on august 15, 1947**, india became independent.

at that same moment, a new country called **pakistan** — a region with a muslim majority — became its own nation.


&nbsp;


the border between the two countries was drawn in a rush. it cut through villages, families, and lives. millions of people had to move, often by force, from one side to the other.


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## balancing between two sides in the cold war


&nbsp;


india's location made it **strategically important**.

it bordered both the soviet union and china. the western powers took great interest in what india would do.


&nbsp;


the united kingdom kept trading with india. it supplied weapons and trained indian military officers. that old bond did not just vanish overnight.


&nbsp;


india's first prime minister was **jawaharlal nehru**.

he wanted to keep his distance from the united states. here is why:


&nbsp;


- he felt a pull toward **socialism** as a way of running a country.
- he had visited the soviet union and seen how it worked up close.
- he recognized the people's republic of china as the true government of china.
- he was angry that the united states refused to let the people's republic of china take china's seat on the united nations security council.
- he watched how the united states treated its own citizens of color, and it made him **suspicious** of american intentions toward india.


&nbsp;


still, nehru did not shut any door.

in the 1950s, he accepted aid from the united states.

he also took aid from the soviet union.

he played both sides carefully.


&nbsp;


india helped start something called the **non-aligned movement**. it was a group of countries that refused to pick a side in the cold war. like indonesia, india stayed free of those entangling ties. it wanted to chart its own course.


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## the problem with china's border


&nbsp;


in **1950**, india's relationship with china changed.

china set up border posts in tibet to mark where its territory ended and another country's began. some of that land was also claimed by india.


&nbsp;


at first, both countries tried hard to stay friendly.

in 1954, they signed a treaty called the **five principles of peaceful coexistence**. it was meant to keep things calm between them.


&nbsp;


then things broke down.


&nbsp;


in **1959**, an uprising against chinese rule erupted in tibet. the fourteenth dalai lama, tibet's spiritual and political leader, fled across the border and asked india for protection.

india said yes. it gave him shelter.

china was furious.


&nbsp;


in **1962**, a confrontation along that disputed border with tibet turned into full war between india and china.


&nbsp;


during the fighting:


&nbsp;


- the united states sent aid to india.
- the soviet union said it would stay neutral. but behind the scenes, it kept sending money and technology to india anyway.
- that quiet choice **deepened the growing split** between the soviet union and china. two countries that had once stood together were drifting apart.


&nbsp;

