# european migration in the 1800s and early 1900s


## table of contents



- who left europe?

- where did they go?

- what work did immigrants do?

- how were immigrants received?

- new immigration laws in the united states

- a different story in latin america



&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## who left europe?



&nbsp;


about 60 million people left europe between the 1800s and early 1900s. they wanted better work and more money on other continents.

&nbsp;


most came from poor farming areas. southern italy and sicily were common starting points. these regions had very little industry or trade.

&nbsp;


about 28 million of these migrants went to the united states. most arrived between 1890 and 1914. the majority were peasant farmers from southern and eastern europe.

&nbsp;


germany and ireland also sent many immigrants. in fact, those two countries led immigration in the first half of the 1800s.

&nbsp;


not everyone left for money. some fled religious persecution. jewish people in the russian empire faced discrimination and anti-semitism. this often turned into violent attacks called pogroms. people were killed. property was destroyed.

&nbsp;


others who emigrated included:



- political dissidents

- craftspeople displaced by industrialization



&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## where did they go?



&nbsp;


after the united states, these countries received the most european immigrants:



- argentina

- brazil

- chile

- uruguay

&nbsp;


about 4.5 million emigrants went to argentina. at one point, they made up 30 percent of its population. latin america attracted many italians and germans. it also drew spanish and portuguese settlers.

&nbsp;


canada received about 5.1 million people between the 1850s and the 1920s.

&nbsp;


other common destinations included:



- australia

- new zealand

- south africa

&nbsp;


gold discoveries made some places especially attractive. gold was found in australia in 1851. it was found in south africa in 1886.

&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## what work did immigrants do?



&nbsp;


european immigrants took on many different jobs once they arrived. here is a breakdown by group:



- **many** worked in factories.

- **jewish people** were especially active in the garment industry, such as in new york city.

- **polish and other eastern europeans** worked in steel mills in pittsburgh and in chicago's stockyards.

- **portuguese** often worked in fishing. in new england, they also worked alongside french canadians in textile mills.

- **italians** did a variety of work: agriculture, mining, and building trades.

- **welsh people** found jobs as miners in the west.

- **irish immigrants** worked in mines, built railroads, and labored in factories across the united states.

- **women** from all these groups entered domestic service.



&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## how were immigrants received?



&nbsp;


the response to newcomers often depended on their ethnicity.

&nbsp;


in the united states:



- the "foreign" ways of germans and eastern european christians might be mocked. but they were largely accepted.

- irish newcomers faced rejection at first. most were roman catholics. they arrived in large numbers in the 1840s and 1850s. the united states was mostly protestant then. by the second half of the 1800s, though, irish people and their children had become politicians, business owners, and police officers.

- jewish people and italians were more recent arrivals. they often faced discrimination. anti-semitism was widespread.



&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## new immigration laws in the united states



&nbsp;


by the end of the 1800s, many native-born protestants of northern european ancestry called for changes to immigration laws.

&nbsp;


in **1924**, congress passed the **johnson-reed act**. this law did two main things:



1. it set a **quota** on the total number of immigrants allowed into the united states from outside the western hemisphere.

2. it limited each nationality's share to **2 percent** of that group already living in the united states in 1890.

&nbsp;


this meant a person could be turned away if their nationality's quota was full. this happened even if the overall limit had not been reached.

&nbsp;


why did this matter? before 1890, most immigrants came from northern and western europe. fewer came from southern or eastern europe. so the 2 percent rule allowed far more english, irish, and german people to enter than italian, polish, or russian jewish people.

&nbsp;


immigration from **asia** was banned. the only exception was the united states territory of the philippines.

&nbsp;


---



&nbsp;


## a different story in latin america



&nbsp;


latin america told a very different story.

&nbsp;


argentina and uruguay had never had large populations. why? they lacked their neighbors' mineral wealth. their climates were not suited to growing sugar or coffee. so european immigrants were a welcome boost there.

&nbsp;


brazil and venezuela took a deliberate approach. they sought out european immigrants on purpose. their goal was to make their predominantly african, native american, and biracial population more white.

# table of contents



the zimmermann telegram

why mexico could not join

the mexican revolution begins (1910 to 1913)

the factions fight (1913 to 1914)

villa's raid and the response (1916)

the united states reacts and enters the war



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the zimmermann telegram



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

on **march 1, 1917**, a secret message was made public.

it was called the **zimmermann telegram**.

this event made tensions between germany and the united states even worse.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

germany's foreign minister, **arthur zimmermann**, had sent this message to mexico.

he offered mexico a deal.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## what happened before that



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1916**, there were many attacks along the border between america and mexico.

a man named **francisco "pancho" villa** was behind them.

he attacked towns in the united states.

the american army sent thousands of troops to the border.

their job: track down villa.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## germany's plan



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

germany saw a chance.

it wanted mexico to cause more trouble at the border if war broke out with the united states.

more attacks would mean more american troops stuck at the border.

those troops could not be sent to fight in europe.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in return, germany made a promise.

if germany won the war, it would give mexico back three regions: **texas**, **new mexico**, and **arizona**.

mexico had lost that land in **1848** after a war with the united states.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# why mexico could not join



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in reality, mexico could not take part in this plan.

even if it wanted to, it was too divided.

in **1917**, violent fighting raged among many groups inside mexico.

villa's forces were just one of them.

to understand why, we need to look back at how the trouble started.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the mexican revolution begins (1910 to 1913)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1910**, a liberal politician named **francisco madero** ran for president of mexico.

he challenged **porfirio díaz**.

díaz had held the presidency without a break since **1884**.

his government ruled like a dictatorship.

it favored wealthy landowners and business owners.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## the rigged election



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

díaz won the election.

the vote was rigged.

he then had madero arrested.

this act sparked uprisings across the country.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1911**, díaz was forced to step down.

he left the country.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## madero's short rule



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

madero was declared president.

but he failed to deliver the changes people expected.

a new revolt broke out against his government.

in **1913**, madero was forced to give up his office.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

a general named **victoriano huerta** replaced him.

huerta commanded rebel forces within the mexican army.

soon after taking power, he had madero killed.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

huerta tried to rule like a dictator.

opposition rose quickly.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the factions fight (1913 to 1914)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in the south, **emiliano zapata** led a rebellion.

his followers were indigenous farmers.

they wanted land reform.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in the north, three men led the revolution:

- **venustiano carranza**
- **álvaro obregón**
- **pancho villa**

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## america steps in



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

american president **woodrow wilson** backed carranza.

he supplied carranza's forces with weapons and supplies.

wilson also used this moment to order american marines into mexico.

his stated goal: protect property owned by americans.

in **april 1914**, the american navy took control of the mexican port of **veracruz**.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## huerta falls



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

throughout the summer of **1914**, the rebel groups fought each other.

each leader wanted to replace huerta.

in **july**, huerta fled into exile.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

carranza declared himself president.

villa and zapata objected.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# villa's raid and the response (1916)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **march 1916**, villa attacked the town of **columbus** in new mexico.

this was payback for american support of carranza.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

wilson responded by sending **five thousand troops** across the border.

their mission: capture villa.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

villa escaped them.

within a year, the american troops had gone home.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## the fallout



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

this invasion damaged relations between the united states and carranza.

he refused to accept what he saw as an insult to mexico's right to rule itself.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

by **1917**, carranza was firmly in power.

but rebellion continued.

villa led fighters in the northern state of **chihuahua**.

zapata led fighters in **morelos** in the south.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the united states reacts and enters the war



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

so when germany approached carranza with its offer of an alliance against the united states, he was not in a position to accept it.

he did not have the control or the stability to do so.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

but when the telegram became public, the united states was furious.

the country was not yet at war.

and germany was already planning how to break up its territory.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## money and business push toward war



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

american banks had lent money to the european allies.

if germany won, those loans would go unpaid.

so the banks supported american involvement.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

businesses saw a different benefit.

they hoped that if the united states helped the allies win, the allies would give them greater access to global markets.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## public anger grows



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

popular support for going to war against germany grew fast.

the country was already angry over the sinking of the **lusitania** and the **sussex**.

the zimmermann telegram added fuel to that fire.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **april 1917**, the united states entered the conflict on the side of the allied powers.

&nbsp;


# table of contents


&nbsp;


**1.** adapting to a long war


**2.** shells and politics


**3.** pressure on the british government


**4.** the united states and war production


**5.** paying for the war


**6.** recruiting soldiers


**7.** russia's industrial shortcomings


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 1. adapting to a long war


&nbsp;


the war was **long**.

it touched every part of life.

every country had to change how it worked at home.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


in britain and france, many young men joined the army.

some volunteered.

others were forced to serve through **conscription**.

this meant skilled workers left their factories.

factories needed new workers.

training those workers took time.

production slowed down.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


germany faced a different problem.

the allied blockade cut off its raw materials.

germany had to find new sources.

it could not rely on the old supply routes.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 2. shells and politics


&nbsp;


production problems became **political** problems.

in 1915, british commanders made an accusation.

they said they were turned back in an attack.

the reason was a lack of artillery shells.

this was called the **"shell crisis."**


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


it raised a hard question.

who was to blame?

had the government not ordered enough shells?

or had the workers not worked hard enough?


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


the british government blamed the army.

it said the military had not placed large enough orders.

shell output then rose in 1915.

this happened in britain and in every other combatant nation.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 3. pressure on the british government


&nbsp;


the british government faced **heavy** pressure during the war.

the liberal party was in power.

but this party had always been against war.

world war one threw the party into confusion.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


the shell crisis hurt the government's image.

so did the failures at gallipoli.

both made the government look **weak**.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


david lloyd george added to the tension.

he served as minister of munitions.

he was also a liberal.

he kept maneuvering against the prime minister.

these divisions grew until the end of the war.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 4. the united states and war production


&nbsp;


the united states entered the war later.

its government moved to control industry in a new way.

it created the **war industries board**.

barnard baruch, a financier, ran the board.

the board controlled prices.

it organized production to support the war effort.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


but the united states had never used central planning before.

it had no experience with centralized economic control.

so it could not command war production the way britain could.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


the war industries board made some progress.

but it never fully dictated terms to industry.

when the war ended, the board was shut down right away.

still, its creation pointed toward the government controls that would become necessary in world war two.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 5. paying for the war


&nbsp;


most national governments struggled to **finance** the war.

taxation covered less than one-third of the cost.

the rest came from other sources:


&nbsp;


- selling **war bonds** to the public


- printing more money


- taking on additional debt


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


these choices caused price inflation to hit new highs.

debt, extra money in circulation, and product shortages all squeezed civilian budgets at once.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 6. recruiting soldiers


&nbsp;


the british army needed more troops.

it launched active recruitment campaigns.

one popular tactic was the **"pals" unit**.

young men signed up together as a group.

they were guaranteed to serve in the same unit.

this made enlistment feel like a shared adventure among friends.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


but there was a **dark side**.

a whole neighborhood or village could lose nearly all its young men in one battle.

the practice ended after the somme campaign in 1916.

that campaign caused very high casualties.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;



## 7. russia's industrial shortcomings


&nbsp;


the war exposed how far behind russia still was in **industrializing**.

its railroads were not enough.

they could not move troops to the front and food to the cities at the same time.

factory workers went hungry.


&nbsp;
&nbsp;


arms manufacturers fell short too.

they could not make enough guns or bullets for the soldiers.

this lack of equipment hurt troop morale badly.

soldiers felt their government did not support them.

they began to question its decisions.

