# 1 &nbsp;&nbsp; The Long Conflict Between Ireland and Britain

&nbsp;

Tensions between Ireland and Britain go back many centuries.

&nbsp;

Here is where it all started:

&nbsp;

- In the twelfth century, England took control of Ireland.

&nbsp;

- That one act set off hundreds of years of fighting.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 2 &nbsp;&nbsp; The Fight for Home Rule

&nbsp;

In the early 1900s, one issue ruled politics on both sides.

&nbsp;

That issue was **home rule**.

&nbsp;

## What would home rule mean?

&nbsp;

- Ireland would get its own parliament.

&nbsp;

- It could handle its own local matters.

&nbsp;

- It would no longer take orders from London.

&nbsp;

Support for this idea grew fast.

&nbsp;

By the time of the war, the British government had even partly approved it.

&nbsp;

## Who said no?

&nbsp;

Not everyone wanted home rule.

&nbsp;

In Northern Ireland, **protestant unionists** refused.

&nbsp;

Here is why:

&nbsp;

- They wanted to stay in the union with Britain.

&nbsp;

- They feared a Catholic-led Irish government.

&nbsp;

- They worried about unfair treatment if home rule passed.

&nbsp;

The British government put the question on hold.

&nbsp;

It needed all its energy for the war.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 3 &nbsp;&nbsp; The Easter Rising

&nbsp;

Irish nationalists grew tired of waiting.

&nbsp;

Some wanted more than home rule.

&nbsp;

They wanted a fully free Ireland.

&nbsp;

## A link with Germany

&nbsp;

This cause pushed Ireland toward Germany.

&nbsp;

Both nations shared one enemy: Britain.

&nbsp;

Talks began between them.

&nbsp;

The **Irish Republican Brotherhood** planned an uprising.

&nbsp;

Their plan relied on German weapons arriving in time.

&nbsp;

But the weapons never came.

&nbsp;

The Brotherhood went ahead anyway.

&nbsp;

## What happened

&nbsp;

On **April 24, 1916**, the Easter Rising began.

&nbsp;

It lasted several days.

&nbsp;

Here is a quick summary:

&nbsp;

- Irish nationalists declared the birth of the **Irish Republic**.

&nbsp;

- They took key buildings in Dublin.

&nbsp;

- Britain sent in its army.

&nbsp;

- Fighting broke out between the two sides.

&nbsp;

- Thousands were killed or hurt, many of them civilians.

&nbsp;

## How it ended

&nbsp;

In the end, the Irish fighters surrendered.

&nbsp;

They could not stand against a trained army.

&nbsp;

The punishment was harsh:

&nbsp;

- Many leaders of the rising were executed.

&nbsp;

- Others got long prison sentences.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 4 &nbsp;&nbsp; What Came After: A Divided Island

&nbsp;

## The South

&nbsp;

The British response pushed young Irish people toward radical ideas.

&nbsp;

The executions made the cause feel more urgent than ever.

&nbsp;

After the war, the southern counties joined together.

&nbsp;

They formed the **Irish Free State**.

&nbsp;

Today, that state is called the **Republic of Ireland**.

&nbsp;

## The North

&nbsp;

The northern counties chose a different path.

&nbsp;

- They stayed in the union.

&nbsp;

- They remain part of the United Kingdom to this day.

