&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **1. the wish for freedom after world war two**


&nbsp;

after **world war two** ended, the peoples of africa wanted to be free from western control.

they were not alone in this wish. the vietnamese felt the same way. they also wanted their own country to govern itself.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **2. a promise made in 1941**


&nbsp;

in **1941**, the united kingdom and the united states signed a document called the **atlantic charter**.

this agreement set out shared goals for the world after the war. it promised that all peoples should be able to choose their own government. this idea is called **self-determination**.

african countries wanted that promise to become real.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **3. the pan-african congress in manchester**


&nbsp;

in **october 1945**, a large meeting took place in **manchester, england**. it was the fifth gathering of the **pan-african congress**.

delegates came from many places:

- europe's african colonies
- the caribbean
- the united states
- india
- sri lanka
- central america


&nbsp;

together, they called for an end to **colonialism**. they wanted every colonised people to be free.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **4. britain's slow move toward change in ghana**


&nbsp;

the european colonial powers did not agree with this call. world war two had badly damaged their economies. some leaders saw african resources and markets as essential to rebuilding their countries.

in **1946**, britain gave its colony of **gold coast** (now called **ghana**) a new constitution. this showed that the united kingdom wanted to keep control over its african possessions. like other colonial powers, it was not ready to let go.

the new constitution did give africans more chances to elect members of a legislative council. but the council could only advise. it had no real power. all true authority stayed with the colony's appointed governor.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **5. nkrumah and the road to ghana's independence**


&nbsp;

in **august 1947**, ghanaians formed a new political group called the **united gold coast convention**. this group asked the united kingdom to make the colony self-governing. **kwame nkrumah** was one of its founding members.

in **january 1948**, nkrumah and another member of that group spoke to a gathering of ghanaians who had served in the military. like many in the gold coast, these men were angry. the government had failed to fix unemployment and rising prices. they planned to write a formal complaint to the governor.

a few days later, **riots** broke out across the colony. the government blamed the united gold coast convention. it arrested several members, including nkrumah.

in **1949**, nkrumah founded his own political party called the **convention people's party**. in january 1950, he called on his followers to begin a general strike. his goal was to force the british to allow the drafting of a new constitution. he was jailed again.

while in prison, nkrumah won an election in **1951**. this was the first election in africa that allowed every adult to vote, regardless of race or gender. nkrumah became ghana's prime minister.

on **march 6, 1957**, ghana received its independence.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **6. kenya's fight for freedom**


&nbsp;

after ghana, other british colonies also won their freedom.

in **1944**, a group called the **kenya african union** was founded. it called for kenya's liberation from british rule. in 1947, **jomo kenyatta** took over as its leader.

the united kingdom did not want to grant independence to kenya. many white settlers owned profitable coffee plantations there. they controlled more land than black africans did.

in **1952**, a group called the **kenya land and freedom army** began fighting for independence. people commonly knew them by another name: the **mau mau**. the mau mau was a nationalist movement among the kikuyu people. it used violence to reach its goal.

to spread fear, the mau mau attacked civilians. they targeted both white and black people. they burned people to death or killed them with machetes. africans who stayed loyal to the british government were also targeted.

the united kingdom responded harshly. it banned all kenyan political parties. this included the kenya african union, which did not advocate violence. the british struck back at the mau mau. thousands of people were sent to **internment camps**. entire villages were forcibly moved to new areas. the stated reason was to protect them from the mau mau. innocent civilians were killed by forces employed by the british government.

by **1956**, the uprising had largely ended. in 1960, the united kingdom began talks about kenyan independence. independence was granted in **1963**. kenyatta became the country's prime minister.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **7. france and its african colonies**


&nbsp;

nationalism and the desire for independence were strong in colonies of other european countries too. some imperial powers tried moderate changes at first.

in **1946**, france proposed sweeping reforms for its african colonies. it ended forced labor. it ended racially discriminatory practices. it allowed a degree of self-rule. but it did not offer independence. that was what france's african subjects most wanted. they fought for it.

from **1947 to 1949**, the **malagasy revolt** raged in madagascar. it cost thousands of lives.

in **cameroon**, violence ended colonialism in 1957.

in **algeria**, warfare lasted from **1954 to 1962**. france granted independence in 1962. more than one million people of european descent left the country.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **8. the belgian congo and cold war tensions**


&nbsp;

in the **belgian congo** (now called the democratic republic of the congo), **cold war tensions** added to the violence.

in **1960**, belgium promised the congolese independence in thirty years. nationalist groups rejected that timeline. two major groups said they wanted independence immediately:

- the **congolese national movement**
- the **alliance of bakongo**


&nbsp;

black congolese held protests. these often turned into riots. they refused to pay taxes. many white residents grew alarmed. they started citizens' militias. these militias attacked black residents.

belgium saw the strength of the nationalist protest. it agreed to grant congo its independence on **june 30, 1960**.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **9. what happened after congo gained independence**


&nbsp;

the violence did not stop after independence.

**émile janssens** was the white commander of the force publique. this was the belgian congo's security force. a few days after independence, he told black troops that white officers would remain in charge. soldiers mutinied.

**patrice lumumba** led the congolese national movement. he had been elected prime minister. he replaced janssens with a black commander. the force was renamed the **national congolese army**. lumumba promoted black soldiers.

this did not end the mutiny. white people were attacked across the country. belgian forces invaded to protect them.

in the chaos, congo's mineral-rich southern province of **katanga** broke away. it proclaimed its own independence. lumumba appealed to the **united nations**. the united nations had sent peacekeeping forces to restore order. he asked it to end katanga's secession. the united nations refused. it said secession was an internal political matter. it would not interfere.

lumumba then asked the **united states** for help. his request was denied. finally, he turned to the **soviet union**. the soviets provided weapons and military advisers. in western eyes, this branded lumumba a communist.

colonel **joseph-désiré mobutu** (also known as mobutu sese seko) was the new head of the security force. he took advantage of the chaos. he put lumumba under house arrest. he placed **joseph kasa-vubu** in charge of the government. kasa-vubu led the alliance of bakongo.

lumumba was later killed by katangese troops. the **central intelligence agency** knew about this and supported it. the agency provided money and weapons to both mobutu and kasa-vubu.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **10. britain's "wind of change" speech**


&nbsp;

british politicians watched the strength of nationalist fervor in kenya and other colonies. they feared more violence like that seen in congo. so they moved quickly to liberate british colonies in africa, the caribbean, and asia.

in **1960**, **harold macmillan**, the british prime minister, visited africa. he gave a speech called **"the wind of change."** he declared that this wind was sweeping across africa. the time of liberation had come. this was true even if white britons did not want it.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **11. portugal's refusal to let go**


&nbsp;

unlike britain, france, and belgium, **portugal refused to give up its colonies**.

the second portuguese republic held power from **1933**. its government was nationalistic and close to fascist in style. it regarded the colonies as part of portugal itself. it saw portugal as a guarantor of order and civilization in africa.

independence movements sprang up in **angola**, **portuguese guinea** (now called guinea-bissau), and **mozambique**. they also appeared elsewhere in africa. some groups wanted self-government modeled on western democracies. others favored a communist system.

in angola, portuguese troops fought three separate groups:


- the **national liberation front of angola**. this was a largely rural organization made up of bakongo people.

- the **marxist people's movement for the liberation of angola**. this was a largely urban group.

- the **national union for the total independence of angola**. this was a rural group made up mainly of ovimbundu people.


&nbsp;

in mozambique, the marxist **mozambique liberation front** fought for independence. in guinea-bissau, the **african party for the independence of guinea and cape verde** led the struggle. it was also a marxist organization.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **12. the end of portuguese rule in africa**


&nbsp;

the cold war entered the fight in the portuguese colonies.

the soviet union, china, romania, cuba, and yugoslavia all gave aid to the independence groups. the united states did not provide aid. portugal was a fellow member of the **north atlantic treaty organization** alliance.

in **1974**, leftist military officers in portugal overthrew the government of the second portuguese republic. they established a democratic government. this new government ended the war with the african colonies. it granted independence to angola, guinea-bissau, and mozambique. it also granted independence to **cape verde** and **são tomé and príncipe**. these are islands off the african coast.

independence did not end the violence. in **1975**, civil war broke out in angola. factions fought for control of the country. the united states supported two of the three groups in their struggle against the marxist group that had won power.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **13. rhodesia, now zimbabwe**


&nbsp;

violence also erupted in the former british colony of **rhodesia** (now called **zimbabwe**).

white residents rejected the british government's plans for majority rule. they did not want a government controlled by black rhodesians. in **1965**, they proclaimed independence from the united kingdom on their own terms. **ian smith** became prime minister. britain opposed this move.

civil war broke out between the white government and two black nationalist groups:


- the **zimbabwe african people's union**, led by **joshua nkomo**.

- the **zimbabwe african national union**, led by **robert mugabe**.


&nbsp;

the soviet union supported the first group. china supported the second group. eventually, all three parties reached an agreement. in **1980**, the united kingdom officially recognized zimbabwe's independence. robert mugabe became prime minister.

thousands of nkomo's followers were tortured, imprisoned, or killed. this continued until nkomo and mugabe combined their political parties in **1987**.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **14. south africa and apartheid**


&nbsp;

the situation in south africa was similar to rhodesia. black africans there struggled for independence not against a european power. they struggled against white africans who lived in the country.

in **1909**, the british cape colony, natal, and the boer republics of transvaal and the orange free state were combined. together they formed a self-governing dominion within the british empire. only the white minority was allowed to rule. the contest for power was between whites of british descent and **afrikaners** (of dutch descent).

laws denied black africans the right to vote. they also prevented black people from claiming most of the country's land. black south africans were barred from living in some areas. many were forcibly removed to "homelands" set apart from white communities.

in **1948**, discriminatory practices and racial segregation were formalized into a system called **apartheid**. in 1950, south africa's legislature passed the **suppression of communism act**. this law banned the anti-apartheid south african communist party. it also banned any actions aimed at ending racially discriminatory practices.

in **1960**, the united kingdom allowed south africans to vote on whether to remain a dominion. the majority of the population could not participate in that vote. in 1961, south africa officially became a republic. but the true liberation movement was still underway.


&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## **15. the fight against apartheid**


&nbsp;

from its very beginning, groups like the **african national congress** and the **pan-africanist congress** fought apartheid. at first, they used peaceful protest.

in **1960**, police killed peaceful protesters in the black township of **sharpeville**. the government then banned both the african national congress and the pan-africanist congress. after this, anti-apartheid activists began using sabotage. they attacked police and military targets. they also continued peaceful tactics like strikes.

in **1976**, protests erupted in black townships. a new law required black african students to learn afrikaans. this was the language spoken by south africa's dutch colonizers. in the township of **soweto**, police opened fire on schoolchildren. more than one hundred were killed.

over decades, thousands of activists were arrested and imprisoned. **nelson mandela**, a leader of the african national congress, was among them. many others were executed.

by the **1980s**, continued opposition to apartheid began to have an effect. foreign countries imposed economic sanctions. people boycotted south african businesses. the world condemned the regime.

in **1991**, south africa officially ended the policy of apartheid. in **1994**, the first elections were held in which all south africans could vote regardless of race. the african national congress swept to victory. the new national assembly elected nelson mandela president.

