# the final year of the war



&nbsp;

## germany's early successes



&nbsp;

the final year of the war started well for Germany.


in March, German troops launched an offensive against the British. they quickly gained the advantage. poor weather and fog caused confusion on the battlefield. the British may have retreated farther than they needed to.


Germany moved toward Paris again. it resumed bombing the city, just as it had in the early weeks of the war. Germany also signed treaties with Russia and Romania. both countries left the war. they gave up territory in the process.


by summer 1918, Germany held more land than at any point before.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the allies gain numbers



&nbsp;

in that same summer, a large force of United States troops finally arrived. after four long years of war, France and Britain welcomed the help. China and Greece also joined the war. Brazil entered in October 1917.


other nations declared war on the Central Powers too. Haiti, Cuba, and most of Central America did so between spring 1917 and summer 1918. none of these countries sent troops. none played a decisive role in the fighting.


the Central Powers had done well in the first half of 1918. but now the Allies had a huge numerical edge. their advantage was nearly three to one. the Central Powers could not hold out against that for long.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the meuse-argonne offensive



&nbsp;

the Allies began offensive moves. they won some victories. these wins set the stage for what would happen in the fall.


the last major battle of the war was the **meuse-argonne offensive** in France. it began in September 1918. Allied leaders had planned this counterattack for many months. hundreds of thousands of United States troops joined British and French forces. they used infantry attacks, poison gas, tanks, and aircraft.


the Allied armies advanced several miles quickly. the German armies held their ground at first. but over the next month, the tide turned against them.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## germany crumbles



&nbsp;

Germany's situation was bleak. food shortages caused widespread panic and despair. troops began to desert. civil unrest spread across Germany and Austria-Hungary.


German naval commanders wanted one last moment of glory. in late October, they ordered the fleet to sail out and engage the British. the sailors knew there was no chance of victory. they did not want to go on a suicide mission. about one thousand sailors mutinied. they refused to set sail.


in Kiel, home to a major German naval base, both sailors and workers refused orders. the revolt spread to other cities quickly.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## a new government in berlin



&nbsp;

in Berlin, politicians from the far-left Socialist Party saw an opportunity. they used the growing revolt to push for major change. they wanted to restore order. they called for the Kaiser to step down. they demanded a republic.


**Wilhelm II abdicated on November 9, 1918.** he left Germany and went to the Netherlands. he lived there until his death in 1941.


leaders of the more moderate Social Democrats then took charge. they proclaimed a provisional government. Germany became a republic. the German military agreed to serve under this new civilian government.


political leaders then moved forward with **armistice** talks. an armistice is a cease-fire agreement. these negotiations had already begun with the Allies. leaders believed Germany could not win the war. they thought ending it was best for the country. this move also helped isolate the socialist radicals.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the flu pandemic



&nbsp;

another enemy struck in the final months of the war: **influenza**. it hit both the battlefield and the home front. by fall 1918, a flu pandemic was raging across Europe and the United States.


the first cases appeared in the United States earlier that year. the mass movement of troops across the Atlantic helped spread the virus. the flu was very deadly. young adults were hit especially hard. as the pandemic continued without slowing down, tens of millions of people around the world died. the virus finally burned itself out the following year.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the armistice



&nbsp;

Austria signed an armistice on **November 3, 1918**. Hungary followed on **November 13**. for Germany, the armistice took effect at **11:00 a.m. on November 11**. it imposed a cease-fire on all units.


the armies kept fighting right up to that exact minute. then, at the appointed time, the guns went silent. men climbed out of their trenches. they stepped away from behind their batteries.


the Allies chose to request an armistice rather than a surrender. this distinction mattered. a surrender meant one side had to accept defeat. Germany was not ready to do that in 1918. an armistice, however, simply meant a cease-fire while formal negotiations took place. Germany believed those talks would let it keep some of its war gains. it hoped to leave the fighting with honor and dignity.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## terms of the armistice



&nbsp;

under the terms, German troops had to pull out of France and Belgium. they had to return to Germany. the fact that German soldiers were still on foreign soil when the war ended became a point later leaders exploited. Adolf Hitler used it in the coming decade.


Germany also had to hand over its military equipment to the Allies. this included its navy. most of the ships went to **Scapa Flow**, in the Orkney Islands off the coast of Scotland. the following year, German crews scuttled those ships. they sank them rather than let the Allies take them intact.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## the cost of war



&nbsp;

the casualties were staggering. these numbers included soldiers who died or were wounded. they also included prisoners and those listed as missing.


&nbsp;

| country | casualties |
|---|---|
| Russia | more than 9 million |
| France | more than 6 million |
| Germany | over 7 million |
| Austria-Hungary | over 7 million |


&nbsp;

in total, more than **8.5 million soldiers died** in the war. another **21 million were wounded**. the number of civilians who perished or were injured was also in the millions.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## aftermath and treaty talks



&nbsp;

countries were still reeling from the carnage when treaty negotiations began. the talks dragged on for six months after the armistice. Germany turned out to be wrong about what it could expect from those discussions.


there was still much damage and suffering left to recover from. but the war was over.

