# natural rights in the enlightenment


## table of contents



1. what are natural rights?

2. john locke and natural rights

3. the french enlightenment: voltaire and montesquieu

4. rousseau and the social contract

5. critics: bentham and burke

6. the lasting debate



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 1. what are natural rights?


&nbsp;


natural rights are rights that all human beings have.

examples include the right to life and the right to liberty.

in the eighteenth century, many philosophers wrote about these rights. they filled books and sparked long conversations.


&nbsp;


the core idea is this:

every person has basic rights. no human law can take them away. these rights cannot be revoked or rescinded.


&nbsp;


this idea did not start with the enlightenment. it came from much older traditions of justice and natural law. many ancient cultures had their own versions.


&nbsp;


for example, in ancient persian zoroastrianism, there was a concept called "asha."

it meant "god's will."

it described an unchangeable law that comes from the divine. this law governs the whole universe.


&nbsp;


many ancient religious and philosophical traditions developed ideas about natural law. but european enlightenment thinkers did something new. they turned these old ideas into a political system. that was novel at the time.


&nbsp;


in the eighteenth century, people placed more trust in reason. they wanted to improve human life.

this pushed enlightenment philosophers to imagine new political systems.

these systems would be based on natural rights.

they would replace the divine right of kings.

they would also replace traditional christian social hierarchies.



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 2. john locke and natural rights


&nbsp;


one of the first enlightenment thinkers to study natural rights was john locke. he was an english philosopher.

he argued that people have three fundamental rights:


&nbsp;


- life
- liberty
- property


&nbsp;


locke wrote a major work called "two treatises of government." in it, he made two key claims.

first, all people are born free.

second, government should exist only with the consent of the governed. this principle is called popular sovereignty.


&nbsp;


there is a problem here.

locke and his european peers said all humans are equal by nature. but in practice, they supported unequal systems. they backed colonialism. they backed the atlantic slave trade. these systems stripped natural rights from everyone except white men. that is a serious contradiction.



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 3. the french enlightenment: voltaire and montesquieu


&nbsp;


french thinkers also debated the scope of natural rights. two key figures stand out.


&nbsp;


### voltaire


&nbsp;


francois-marie d'arouet, better known as voltaire, was a strong defender of natural rights and freedoms.

he openly criticized three targets:


&nbsp;


- the catholic church
- the aristocracy
- the french monarchy


&nbsp;


he focused on defending religious toleration, freedom of speech, and the value of reason. he wrote about these topics in works like "treatise on tolerance" and "republican ideas."


&nbsp;


his most famous work is "candide," a satire published in 1759. in it, he mocked both established religion and secular government.


&nbsp;


### montesquieu


&nbsp;


montesquieu was voltaire's contemporary. he wrote extensively about the link between laws and rights. his main concern was political liberty.

he argued that a state must separate its powers into three branches:


&nbsp;


- legislative
- executive
- judicial


&nbsp;


this separation keeps any one branch from gaining too much power. it checks the government. he laid out this idea in his 1748 book "the spirit of the laws."



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 4. rousseau and the social contract


&nbsp;


a key tension shaped enlightenment thought.

on one side stood state authority.

on the other side stood individual freedom.

jean-jacques rousseau worked through this tension. his most influential treatise is "the social contract."


&nbsp;


the idea behind a social contract goes back far before the enlightenment. it appears in the work of plato and socrates (fourth and fifth centuries before the common era). it also appears in the second-century before the common era buddhist text "the mahavastu."


&nbsp;


here is the core belief:

individuals give up some natural rights to the state. in return, the state must maintain and protect those rights.


&nbsp;


rousseau added an important point. human communities make interdependence necessary. people must balance individual freedom with the authority of the state.

individuals should be free to choose their own actions. but the government must also limit people's actions when needed. this protects the rights of everyone.


&nbsp;


rousseau also discussed the general will. a state is legitimate only if it follows the will of the whole people. it cannot follow the wishes of a small elite group.



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 5. critics: bentham and burke


&nbsp;


not all enlightenment philosophers accepted natural rights. some doubted whether they existed at all.

two important critics were jeremy bentham and edmund burke.


&nbsp;


### jeremy bentham


&nbsp;


bentham was an english lawyer. he adopted utilitarianism. this is a political philosophy with one main goal: achieve the greatest good for the greatest number of people.


&nbsp;


bentham argued that rights do not exist on their own. they are created by the state. outside civil society, there are no natural rights.

even if a government ignored the general will or natural law, bentham warned against challenging it. he said doing so would only bring chaos and lawlessness.


&nbsp;


### edmund burke


&nbsp;


the irish philosopher edmund burke also rejected popular sovereignty. he did not deny that natural law exists. but he argued something different.

once civil society forms, natural rights become irrelevant. only people with virtue and good judgment should hold political power. they would act in the best interests of the people.

according to burke, ordinary citizens would naturally set aside their selfish desires. they would surrender their individual will for the good of the state.



&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## 6. the lasting debate


&nbsp;


the enlightenment produced many different views on natural rights. thinkers disagreed about where these rights come from and what they mean.

but one thing was clear: people began to think more critically about their relationship with the state. they also questioned whether revolution could ever be legitimate.


&nbsp;


some thinkers, like burke and bentham, defended the supremacy of the state over individual rights.

others, like locke and voltaire, championed natural rights. they believed political liberty must not be interfered with.


&nbsp;


as this argument continued through the enlightenment, it grew wider. it expanded into discussions of social contract theory. these later debates focused on two specific questions:

what makes law ethical?

and what makes a political order legitimate?

&nbsp;


