# women's right to vote


&nbsp;


## the suffrage movement before the war


&nbsp;


for many years, people fought for women's right to vote. this was true in the united states and in britain. both countries saw long campaigns.


&nbsp;


people who pushed for the vote were called suffragists. some took bold action to get attention. they wanted the public to see their cause.


&nbsp;


in 1913, emily davison threw herself in front of a horse at a race in england. the horse belonged to the king. she was killed that day.


&nbsp;


suffragist leaders often went to jail. many refused to eat while locked up. guards forced food into them. all of this drew more eyes to the cause.


&nbsp;


## women and the war effort


&nbsp;


women helped with the war in big ways. they did hard physical work in factories that made weapons and supplies. some served as nurses and medics on the front lines.


&nbsp;


this changed how politicians saw women. many began to think it was fair for women to vote.


&nbsp;


the united states gave women the right to vote in 1920. the government said it was a reward for their war work.


&nbsp;


britain took things one step at a time:


&nbsp;

- in 1918, women over thirty could vote if they owned property or met other rules.
- in 1928, all women aged twenty-one and up could vote. the property rule was gone.
- in 1918, men no longer needed to own property to vote. all men over twenty-one got the right.
- for men serving in the military, the voting age dropped to nineteen.


&nbsp;


## other countries followed


&nbsp;


other nations joined the trend:


&nbsp;

- german women won the vote in 1918.
- poland was a new country at that time. it gave women the right to vote right away.
- some countries still set limits. they required property ownership or being a war widow.
- france and italy fell behind. women there could not vote until 1945.


&nbsp;


in japan, groups of women argued for a say in politics through the 1920s and 1930s. the law changed to let women go to political rallies. but they did not get the right to vote until after world war ii.


&nbsp;


in china, a growing movement for women's rights tied into the may fourth movement. this helped women gain the legal right to own property on their own.


&nbsp;


## individual female voices


&nbsp;


huda sha'arawi started the egyptian feminist union in 1923. she stopped wearing her veil and headscarf in public. before that, she had worked to give girls an education. she also took part in egypt's push for independence from britain. in the 1930s, she became vice president of a women's group based in switzerland.


&nbsp;


zaynab al-ghazali started the muslim women's society in europe in 1936. she argued that women could take part in politics outside the home. she said this was possible while still following islam.


&nbsp;


# minority groups and military service


&nbsp;


## native americans


&nbsp;


several minority groups in the united states hoped that serving in the army would earn them more rights. over eleven thousand native americans joined the military during the war. many were volunteers. they hoped this service would bring them full citizenship. in 1924, the government finally granted it to them.


&nbsp;


## african americans


&nbsp;


african americans also hoped for change. for a long time, unfair laws and racial separation controlled their daily lives. they felt world war i gave them a chance to show they were loyal citizens.


&nbsp;


the united states military was split by race. all-black units sent overseas had a rare chance to see how other places treated black people. in france, they noticed something new. they could walk freely and be accepted. french forces let them fight on the front lines. american units kept them mostly in support roles like carrying supplies.


&nbsp;


there was real hope that life back home would be different after the war.


&nbsp;


## little changed after 1918


&nbsp;


after 1918, many found that not much had shifted. unfair laws were still on the books. veterans were often treated poorly when they came home.


&nbsp;


what was new was the chance to move. in 1917, well-paid factory jobs making war supplies began pulling african american workers and families up north. this movement kept going after the war ended. factories started making everyday goods for civilians. by 1920, more than a million african americans had left the south. people called this the great migration. it continued through the next few decades.


&nbsp;


## racism in the north


&nbsp;


racism was not gone in the north either. the summer of 1919 brought race riots to many cities. in the 1920s, the ku klux klan spread across the whole country. it was a white supremacist group that had formed after the civil war.


&nbsp;


still, racism in the north felt milder than what people faced in the south. better jobs and more schools kept pulling people to cities in the north and midwest.


&nbsp;


## the new negro movement


&nbsp;

new jobs and new neighborhoods drew many into civil rights groups. one key group was the national association for the advancement of colored people. this pulled people into the new negro movement in the 1920s. the movement pushed for:


&nbsp;

- a stronger sense of racial pride
- free cultural expression through art, music, and writing
- economic independence from white employers
- forward-looking politics that challenged old power structures


&nbsp;


one early goal was to push for a federal law against lynching. lynching had become a form of mob violence. its targets were almost always african americans. activists knew that white men who killed black people would not face justice in southern state courts. by making lynching a crime tried in a federal court, they hoped to get fairer outcomes.


&nbsp;


congress voted on an anti-lynching bill in 1922. it was rejected. the bill came back in 1935 and was rejected again. white southern politicians blocked any federal action, even when lives were at stake.


&nbsp;


# the harlem renaissance and négritude


&nbsp;


## the harlem renaissance


&nbsp;


racial pride also shaped the artists of the harlem renaissance. this was a burst of african american writing, art, and music. it took its name from a neighborhood in new york city. many people who moved north during the great migration settled there.


&nbsp;


the harlem renaissance reached its peak in the 1920s. it brought the work of many african american writers into the mainstream. key figures included:


&nbsp;

- poet langston hughes
- zora neale hurston, who wrote novels, short stories, and plays about the racial struggles of twentieth-century african americans


&nbsp;


the music from harlem and other black communities featured bold blues and jazz styles. both became world-wide genres. they gave a soundtrack to the fast changes of the decade after world war i.


&nbsp;


## the négritude movement


&nbsp;


the harlem renaissance inspired another movement abroad. in particular, the poems of claude mckay, who was born in jamaica, had a strong impact. this helped spark the négritude movement. it was a literary movement that grew in french-speaking parts of africa and the americas.


&nbsp;


the movement was founded by léopold sédar senghor, a poet and university professor from senegal. the word "négritude" came from aimé césaire, a poet and playwright from martinique. at that time, martinique was a french colony in the caribbean.


&nbsp;


the movement called on black people to reject european culture and values. it urged them to embrace their own african heritage instead.

# Table of Contents

- A New Role for Scientists
- Radar, Electronics, and Early Computers
- Medical Advances That Saved Lives
- The Race to Build the Atomic Bomb
- The Trinity Test

&nbsp;

## A New Role for Scientists

World War Two changed science and technology very fast.

These changes soon became matters of life and death.

Total war also changed the link between governments and scientists.

&nbsp;

Governments poured money into new weapons and tools. They built both deadly and non-deadly technologies. These tools became essential to the war effort and to national security.

&nbsp;

In the fall of 1939, British scientists made a sharp turn. They moved away from pure research. Instead, they focused on work that would help win the war.

&nbsp;

## Radar, Electronics, and Early Computers

Scientists learned more about radio waves. This led to radar. Radar gave troops advance warning of enemy attacks.

&nbsp;

Other advances brought better electronics into military use. These included microwaves and early digital computing devices. After the war, these technologies spread into everyday life. They laid the foundation for the commercial electronics we use today.

&nbsp;

Allied forces also worked hard to break Nazi spy codes. This effort inspired the first basic computers.

&nbsp;

Alan Turing was a British mathematician. He led a team of scientists working for the British Secret Service. Their goal was to crack the Enigma code. The Nazis believed this code was unbreakable.

&nbsp;

Turing's team built an electromagnetic machine. It could cycle through hundreds of thousands of possible key and letter combinations in intercepted Nazi messages. In the end, they broke the code.

&nbsp;

Another major breakthrough was a computer called ENIAC (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer). The United States Army first developed it to calculate artillery trajectories.

&nbsp;

ENIAC ran at electronic speed, not mechanical speed. By 1955, ENIAC had completed more calculations than all humans had ever solved up to that point.

&nbsp;

## Medical Advances That Saved Lives

The war also brought life-saving medical progress. One major development was penicillin. Scientists improved how penicillin was used and mass-produced it on a large scale.

&nbsp;

Another key advance came from Doctor Charles Drew, an African American physician. He developed a process for making blood plasma. This made it easier to store blood for longer periods. Plasma also served as an effective replacement for whole-blood transfusions.

&nbsp;

However, racial bias interfered with this work. In 1941, the American Red Cross agreed with a War Department decision. They began labeling all blood and plasma as either "white" or "negro."

&nbsp;

Doctor Drew resigned from the National Blood Bank in protest. He called this policy racist and unscientific.

&nbsp;

## The Race to Build the Atomic Bomb

Few developments were more dramatic than the push to build an atomic bomb. This effort mobilized massive scientific and civilian resources.

&nbsp;

In December 1938, two German physicists made a key discovery. Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann accidentally split atoms. They discovered nuclear fission.

&nbsp;

A few months later, the Germans set up a secret weapons program called the Uranium Club. Their goal was to create an atomic bomb.

&nbsp;

Albert Einstein was a German-born physicist who had recently moved to the United States. He saw the danger in Hahn's work. Physicist Leo Szilard wrote a letter warning President Roosevelt about this research. The letter urged the United States to increase its own nuclear efforts. Einstein sent it to the president.

&nbsp;

By the late 1930s, British scientists and others were convinced an atomic bomb was possible. Teams of physicists gathered to begin experiments. Some of these scientists were refugees from Nazi Germany. They studied nuclear chain reactions, which are the catalysts of an atomic explosion.

&nbsp;

In August 1942, the United States government launched its top-secret Manhattan Project. The project spread across dozens of sites in the United States. These included:

- Los Alamos, New Mexico
- Oak Ridge, Tennessee
- Hanford, Washington State

&nbsp;

A total of 600,000 workers joined a frenetic race to build the world's first atomic bomb. Germany and Japan were also trying to build their own bombs.

&nbsp;

The German effort ran into serious problems. Top German scientists had fled the country. Some even helped the Manhattan Project. Also, Hitler chose to fund V2 bombers for the air war with England instead of an atomic bomb.

&nbsp;

In 1941, Japan commissioned physicist Yoshio Nishina to begin work on an atomic bomb. The project was called Ni-Go. But it made little progress. Japan had no information shared by Germany. United States air raids also caused heavy damage and disruption.

&nbsp;

## The Trinity Test

In July 1945, the Manhattan Project finally succeeded. A bomb was detonated in the Trinity test at Alamogordo, New Mexico. This was the first successful trial of an atomic weapon.

&nbsp;

William L. Laurence, the official historian of the project, described the moment:

&nbsp;

> "On that moment hung eternity. Time stood still. Space contracted to a pinpoint. It was as though the earth had opened and the skies split. One felt as though they had been privileged to witness the birth of the world—to be present at the moment of creation when the Lord said: let there be light."

&nbsp;

President Roosevelt had died suddenly in April 1945. Vice President Harry S. Truman took over as president. It fell to Truman to decide whether or not to use the new weapon.

&nbsp;


