# portuguese voyages along the west african coast



## table of contents


1. reaching senegal, gambia, and beyond (1442–1456)


2. cape verde and ghana (around 1456–1471)


3. gold and the fortress at elmina (1481)


4. more forts along the gold coast


5. benin and its trade with portugal (1485 to the 1700s)


6. kongo, são tomé, and wider connections (1485–1510s)


7. a network takes shape



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## reaching senegal, gambia, and beyond (1442–1456)



in **1442**, the portuguese reached the **senegal river**. their voyages kept going from there.



by **1448**, some portuguese sailors had gone up the **gambia river**. they reached a big trading town called **kantara**. kantara sat not far east of what is today **basse santa su**, in the far east of the country. at that time, kantara was a key centre for trade across the sahara desert.



by the mid-1450s, two new contacts had formed:


- trade began with the **wolof state of kajoor** on the coast.


- the **bijagós islands**, off the coast of guinea-bissau, entered talks with european traders.



&nbsp;

the first language used was probably **arabic**. many people in fifteenth-century portugal spoke arabic. granada in spain was still an islamic kingdom at that time. and arabic was widely spoken across much of west africa.



in **1456**, one sailor wrote about the bijagós islands. he said this was the last place where african peoples shared a language with portuguese sailors. this also marked the southernmost reach of arab and trans-saharan trade at that time.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## cape verde and ghana (around 1456–1471)



the **cape verde islands** were first reached, probably in **1456**. capeverdean historians still debate whether people already lived there. the evidence from digging up old sites does not give a clear answer. the islands may have served as a salt store for saharan states.



around **1471**, the portuguese reached the coastline of **ghana**.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## gold and the fortress at elmina (1481)



ghana's coast was different from other areas. it sat close to the **gold mines** in the akan forests. soon, akan traders began selling gold informally to the portuguese.



the portuguese state wanted a share of that trade. in **1481**, they sent a large fleet. they talked with **kwamena ansa**, the ruler at edina (now called **elmina**). the goal was to build a fortress.



&nbsp;

the talks were tense. at one point, the portuguese tried to build their fort on the site of a **holy shrine**. this caused a serious stand-off. in the end, the talks concluded. the fortress at elmina was built. it still stands today on what became known as the **gold coast**.



&nbsp;

later, in the seventeenth century, the **dutch** took over the fortress. over time, the dutch and english became the main european nations trading in ghana.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## more forts along the gold coast



the gold coast had a great deal of trade. many akan rulers wanted to grow their trading connections. the portuguese built forts at other places along the coast, such as **axim** and **komenda**. many of these were finished by **1500**.



over time, more forts appeared. they stood almost every few miles along the gold coast. some focused on the **gold trade**. others, like **anishan**, specialised in exporting corn. european ships had high demand for corn.



&nbsp;

the **slave trade** did not become dominant on the gold coast until the eighteenth century.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## benin and its trade with portugal (1485 to the 1700s)



meanwhile, other rulers from west and west-central africa were making contact with the portuguese.



in **1485**, **oba ozolua** of benin received portuguese visitors. around **1490**, the atlantic trading post of **gwatón** was founded.



&nbsp;

at first, benin sold **malaguetta peppers** to the portuguese. but in **1499**, a sea route to india was opened up. after that, benin's peppers became less wanted in europe.



the portuguese tried to set up a trade in enslaved persons from benin to the gold suppliers at elmina. the oba refused.



&nbsp;

over the sixteenth century, relations between benin and portugal got worse. in the early seventeenth century, the obas renewed links with european traders. the dutch wanted the **cloth** produced in benin.



throughout the seventeenth century, cloth remained benin's main export. **ambassy cloths** were traded regularly to elmina. they also went as far afield as brazil.



&nbsp;

by the late seventeenth century, demand for benin cloth fell. imports of luxury cloths from india grew. by the eighteenth century, benin's obas had to turn to the trade in enslaved persons. this was needed to keep and renew the power of edo and the benin state.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## kongo, são tomé, and wider connections (1485–1510s)



the portuguese continued south after benin. they reached the mouth of the **kongo river**. there, they set up relations with the **manikongo** at mbanza kongo, the capital of kongo. his name was **nzika nkuwu**.



by **1485**, the island of **são tomé** had been settled. many baKongo peoples were taken there as enslaved persons. they worked on sugar plantations.



&nbsp;

in **1491**, nzika nkuwu converted to christianity. he took the name **joao i**. this created a link between kongo and elmina through são tomé. the island traded with both places.



by the **1510s**, traders from benin were mentioned as present at the kongolese port of **mpinda**. a new manikongo, **afonso i** (ruling 1509 to 1546), wrote about this in a letter in portuguese.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

## a network takes shape



by the **1510s**, many different peoples and rulers across west africa had established connections with the portuguese. some places, like benin and elmina, also had links further south to kongo.



african rulers hoped to grow their trading contacts. at times, they also sought military support against rivals. in **1516**, benin fought a major battle against nupe with portuguese support. civil wars in jolof and kongo also drew on outside connections.

# table of contents



1. the ghana empire

2. trade made ghana powerful

3. what arab visitors saw

4. the fall of ghana

5. the songhay empire

6. how songhay made money



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



# the ghana empire



ghana was one of the most famous empires in west africa. it was also one of the earliest.


it existed from the 5th century to the 13th century. its land is now part of modern mali and mauritania. trade across the sahara desert shaped its history.


&nbsp;



the capital city was kumbi saleh, in present-day mauritania.


do not confuse this empire with modern ghana. modern ghana has its capital at accra. the country took its name from this older empire.


&nbsp;



the main people of the ghana empire were the serahuli. they are also called soninke. they belonged to the mande-speaking group.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## trade made ghana powerful



ghana grew rich because of its role in trade across the sahara desert. its location was key.


british historian kevin shillington said it clearly:


"ghana's position with regard to the trade made it grow powerful and its rulers became rich. it seems likely that trade was a major factor in the growth of ghana from the very beginning."


&nbsp;



ghana sat halfway between two key goods:


- salt came from the desert to the north.
- gold came from bambuk to the east.


&nbsp;



ghana acted as the middleman. this role brought great wealth.


&nbsp;



the camel changed everything. camels carried heavy loads across the sand. trade between ghana and desert peoples, such as the berbers, grew fast.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## what arab visitors saw



ghana's fame spread because arab traders wrote about it. they recorded what they saw in detail.


in the 11th century, an arab geographer named al-bakari visited kumbi saleh. he described the great wealth he found there. he also noted how well the empire was run.


&nbsp;



he reported that kumbi saleh had two separate areas:


- a foreigners' quarter where arab traders lived.
- the main ward where the king and his people lived.


&nbsp;



al-bakari was amazed by what he saw. he wrote about:


- the king dressed in gold.
- an army of 200,000 men.
- both islam and older beliefs about spirits in nature being practised openly in kumbi saleh.


&nbsp;



these arab writers mostly met kings, nobles, and traders. they cared most about gold. so they wrote little about ordinary people.


still, their writings show that common folk fished and farmed along the river senegal to survive.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## the fall of ghana



ghana rose through trade. it also fell because of trade.


the almoravids began waging war against other berber tribes. trade routes became unsafe. trade dropped.


&nbsp;



dry weather hit hard too. drought made it difficult to feed the people and the large army. the state weakened.


&nbsp;



by the 12th century, smaller kingdoms that answered to ghana, like mali, began to rebel. they wanted freedom from ghana's control.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



# the songhay empire



songhay lasted from the 11th century to the 16th century. it also rose because of trade across the sahara desert.


by the 14th century, muslim traders were settled in gao. gao was the main trade town of songhay. it became the hub for trade across the central and eastern sahara.


&nbsp;



farmers and fishermen in songhay fed the traders well. this kept caravans moving.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## how songhay made money



songhay collected most of its money from taxes on trade caravans.


one great emperor was muhamed ture, also called askia muhamed. he did two important things:


- he introduced islam into songhay.
- he expanded the empire's reach.


&nbsp;



like mansa musa of mali, he made a long journey of faith to mecca. there, he showed how rich and powerful his kingdom was.


&nbsp;



the trade across the sahara desert made songhay rich and well off.

