&nbsp;


# one institution ruled all



after the fall of rome, one institution stayed organized.

that institution was the christian church.

it held together across all of western and eastern europe.

its core beliefs were united and shared by everyone.

but it was also divided into two separate branches.

one branch faced west. the other faced east.

this split was not official at first.

&nbsp;

in 1054, the split became real and permanent.

people call this event the **great schism**.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# two branches, two centers



the **eastern branch** set up its center in constantinople.

it was led by the patriarch of constantinople.

this title was roughly equal to the pope's role in the west.

the eastern church used **greek** for all of its services.

&nbsp;

the **western branch** stayed where it had always been: rome.

it was led by the **pope**.

over time, it became known as the **roman catholic church**.

it kept using **latin** for its prayers and its texts.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# a strict hierarchy



after the split, each branch kept a strict chain of authority.

in the western church, that chain looked like this:

&nbsp;

the **pope** in rome sat at the very top.

he oversaw a large bureaucracy led by **cardinals**.

they were called "princes of the church."

below them came the **archbishops**.

then came the **bishops**.

at the bottom were the **priests**, closest to the people.

&nbsp;

during this time, the roman church became the most powerful
international organization in all of western europe.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# life revolved around the church



farm life depended on the seasons and the weather.

village life and family life depended on the church.

the two ideas fit together naturally.

&nbsp;

the church held special ceremonies called **sacraments**.

these marked every major stage of a person's life:

&nbsp;

birth

growing into adulthood

marriage

burial

&nbsp;

these events brought people back to the church again and again.

there was no escaping its presence in daily life.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# replacing old beliefs



as christianity spread across europe, it replaced older beliefs.

pagan views went away. animistic views went away too.

the church explained supernatural events in its own way.

it explained the forces of nature in its own way as well.

&nbsp;

the new story that people were told was this:

&nbsp;

a **benevolent god** lived up in heaven.

he created the entire universe from nothing.

he existed beyond nature and beyond what people could see.

he controlled all events happening down here on earth.

&nbsp;

he also waged a long war against a force of darkness.

that force was called the **devil**, or **satan**.

satan operated right here on earth, among ordinary people.

in the end, god would defeat him completely.

but until that final day came, satan still had real power.

he could trick humans into doing evil things.

he could lead them to sin against god's will.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# every event had a spiritual meaning



nothing happened by chance in the medieval world.

everything carried a hidden spiritual message.

&nbsp;

if a person got sick, it might mean they had sinned.

if crops failed in the field, it might mean the villagers
had not said their prayers properly that season.

&nbsp;

people who felt guilty became **penitents**.

they went to the priest and confessed all of their sins.

the priest then did two important things:

&nbsp;

1. he **absolved** them. this meant he forgave their sins.

2. he assigned **penance**. this was a task or an act
of self-punishment so they could atone for what they had done.
it was meant to save them from eternal damnation in hell.

&nbsp;

because of this role, the parish priest held enormous power
over his parishioners' lives and their souls.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the pope's power



the pope made final decisions on all matters of **theology**.

he interpreted god's will and shared it with the people.

but his authority did not stop at spiritual questions.

he also held real power over **temporal** matters.

that means everyday, political, and legal affairs too.

&nbsp;

the church could **excommunicate** a person.

this meant cutting them off from the faith entirely.

it meant sending their soul to hell for all eternity.

even kings and queens feared this power deeply.

they dared not challenge the church in any open way.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the seat of all knowledge



the church also held all learning in medieval europe.

there were no universities yet that operated outside it.

&nbsp;

**latin**, the language of the church, served as a unifying force.

europe was made up of many small and isolated regions.

each region had its own local dialect and way of speaking.

people could not always understand one another at all.

latin bridged that gap between every region in europe.

in the early middle ages, nations as we know them today
did not yet exist in any clear or fixed form.

&nbsp;

most serfs could not read or write. they were illiterate.

so they depended entirely on their literate priests.

the priests read the bible for them each week.

the priests interpreted its meaning for them as well.

the bible was understood to be the direct word of god.

without those priests, the serfs had no access to that
word at all. not a single page could they open themselves.

