## 1. the long history of slavery



&nbsp;



slavery is very old. it has existed for thousands of years.


&nbsp;



the ancient greek thinker aristotle made a claim. he said some peoples were "homunculi." this word means "humanlike but not really people." for example, he thought this applied to anyone who did not speak greek.


&nbsp;



both the bible and the koran contain passages about enslaved people. these texts talk about how enslaved people should be treated.


&nbsp;



vikings raided lands from ireland all the way to russia. they brought back enslaved people of many different nationalities.


&nbsp;



during the middle ages, traders worked in the interior of africa. they moved enslaved people along well-established routes. they sold them along the mediterranean coast.


&nbsp;



slavers also brought enslaved europeans to the caribbean. many of these people were children. they were orphaned or homeless. they were captured in the cities of ireland.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## 2. when did slavery become based on race?



&nbsp;



this is a key question. the answer appears to lie in the new world.


&nbsp;



european colonists introduced new crops. these included sugar and coffee. both required hard, tiring work that took great effort.


&nbsp;



the colonists needed more workers. they could not fill this need from prisoners. they also could not rely on indentured servants. so they turned to african laborers.


&nbsp;



the portuguese wanted a trade route to india. but they did something else too. they built forts along the west african coast. the purpose was to export people to europe.


&nbsp;



historians believe that by the year 1500, black enslaved people made up 10 percent of the population in lisbon and seville.


&nbsp;



the catholic church frowned on enslaving christians. because of this, european slave traders expanded their reach further down the coast of africa. they looked for non-christian peoples to enslave.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## 3. racially based slavery in the new world



&nbsp;



europeans settled in brazil, the caribbean, and north america. there, they built a system of slavery based on race.


&nbsp;



the need for a massive labor force was greater here than in western europe. the land was well suited to growing sugar, coffee, rice, and later cotton.


&nbsp;



to meet the ever-growing demand for these crops, large plantations were created.


&nbsp;



these plantations needed a specific kind of labor supply. it had to be:


&nbsp;



- permanent – workers who would stay

- plentiful – enough people to work at scale

- identifiable – easy to recognize and track

- skilled – able to do the hard physical work


&nbsp;



africans fit this need for several reasons. they were already familiar with farming. they also knew animal husbandry well. they had a distinct, identifying skin color. and they could be supplied through the existing african slave trade networks.


&nbsp;



this process set the stage for new world slavery to expand into north america.

# The Founding of Georgia



## Table of Contents



1. How the colony began

2. A vision for a new society

3. The vision fades



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



## 1. How the Colony Began



&nbsp;



New ideas about fairness and reason were spreading across Europe in the early 1700s. By the 1730s, those ideas led to something big: a brand-new colony.



&nbsp;



**Who started it?**



James Oglethorpe was a member of Parliament. He cared deeply about helping people who had fallen on hard times. He visited debtors' prison in London and saw the awful conditions inside. Then he watched what happened when those same people, with no money at all, were set loose on the streets.



&nbsp;



**Getting permission from the king.**



Oglethorpe went to King George II and asked for a charter — a legal document that would let him start a new colony. The king said yes. He saw a good reason: a British colony could sit between South Carolina and Spanish Florida, acting as a shield. In 1732, the king gave the charter to Oglethorpe and twenty other people who shared his goals.



&nbsp;



**The first settlers arrive.**



Oglethorpe led the effort. The colony was named **Georgia**, in honor of the king.

In 1733, Oglethorpe and 113 immigrants stepped off a ship called the **Anne**. Over the next ten years, Parliament paid for another 2,500 settlers to move there. Georgia became the only colonial project ever funded by the government.



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



## 2. A Vision for a New Society



&nbsp;



Oglethorpe's plan was built on the ideas of the Age of Reason. He saw Georgia as a fresh start — a place made for England's "worthy poor," people who had done nothing wrong but still ended up with nothing.



&nbsp;



**What each man got to help him work:**



- 50 acres of land

- Tools

- A full year's worth of supplies



&nbsp;



**The layout of Savannah.**



In the town of Savannah, Oglethorpe drew up a plan for how people would live and work together. The goal was simple: an agrarian model where everyone could feed themselves while treating all men as equals.



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



## 3. The Vision Fades



&nbsp;



Oglethorpe wanted two things banned in Georgia, full stop:

- Alcohol

- Slavery



&nbsp;



**The rules get ignored.**



But colonists kept moving in from other places. Many came from South Carolina. They did not follow the new rules. They brought their old habits with them.



&nbsp;



**What changed by the 1750s.**



The early vision called for a colony built on reason and freedom. By the 1750s, that dream had faded. Georgia was now growing large amounts of rice. Enslaved people grew it and harvested it. The fresh start Oglethorpe had imagined no longer matched what was happening on the ground.

