# who were the federalists?


&nbsp;

the revolution ended british rule in the united states.


but old ideas did not go away.


&nbsp;

supporters of the 1787 federal constitution were called **federalists**.


they kept a very british idea: social hierarchy.


in other words, they believed people had fixed ranks.


&nbsp;

at first, federalists were not a political party.


they simply shared certain beliefs.


&nbsp;

here is what those beliefs looked like:


&nbsp;

voting and holding office depended on owning property.


this kept many citizens out of politics.


&nbsp;

the revolution did **not** change the roles of women and men.


it also did not change the roles of white people and other races.


&nbsp;

people had clear ranks in intelligence and status.


&nbsp;

the idea that all people were equal seemed ridiculous to them.


&nbsp;

federalists argued that women, black people, and native peoples must accept a lower place than white male citizens.


they feared that pushing for equality would destroy the republic.


in their view, the united states was not built to be a democracy.



&nbsp;



# federalists took power


&nbsp;

the men who wrote the constitution wanted to lead the new republic.


and they did.


&nbsp;

they held a majority in the new national government.


as expected, many of them took the first executive posts that congress created.


&nbsp;

here is how president washington filled his cabinet:


&nbsp;

**secretary of the treasury:** alexander hamilton (a leading federalist)


&nbsp;

**secretary of state:** thomas jefferson


&nbsp;

**secretary of war:** henry knox (he had served with washington in the revolutionary war)


&nbsp;

**attorney general:** edmund randolph (a virginia delegate to the constitutional convention)


&nbsp;

in july 1789, congress passed the **judiciary act**.


this law created a supreme court.


it would have six justices.


those justices were committed to the new national government.



&nbsp;



# early laws: taxes and trade


&nbsp;

congress passed its first major law in 1789.


the **tariff act** placed a duty (a tax) on imported goods.


the goal was simple: raise money to fix the country's economic problems.


&nbsp;

this act was a win for nationalists.


nationalists wanted a strong, powerful federal government.


they had tried to pass similar laws during the 1780s under the confederation congress.


but they had failed then.


&nbsp;

congress also taxed foreign ships entering american ports.


the duty was fifty cents per ton.


this was based on the materials being transported, not the weight of the ship itself.


&nbsp;

the purpose was clear: give american ships and goods a commercial advantage over foreign ones.
